eld but 7 tiles that are one tile away from 2 melds and 1 pair (which means you have two incomplete melds each missing one tile, and two singles, and the winning tile completes one meld and forms a pair with one single? That would complete only one meld, not two). Actually, to get 2 melds and 1 pair from 7 tiles with one draw, you need the 7 tiles to be arranged such that the drawn tile either:  \n  a) Completes two melds (if it is a tile that finishes two sequences) and forms a pair with a single, or  \n  b) Completes one meld and forms a pair, and the other meld is already complete in the 7 tiles.  \n\nGiven your tiles, option b is possible if you have a complete meld already. Do you have a complete meld? No, because all are singles or two consecutive. So you don't have a complete meld. So you must rely on option a: the drawn tile completes two melds and forms a pair. That means you have two sequences each missing the same tile, and that tile also pairs with one of your other singles.  \n\nFor example, if you have W2,W3 and B7,B8, and you are waiting for W1 and B6? That's two different tiles, not one. So not possible.  \n\nAlternatively, you could have a pair already in the 7 tiles. Do you have a pair? No.  \n\nSo perhaps I misinterpreted: you might be in a \"ready\" state not because you have a complete waiting pattern, but because you have too many tiles and need to discard to reach 14 tiles. The valid actions are all discards, meaning you are not yet in tenpai; you are choosing which tile to discard to improve toward tenpai.  \n\nYes, that makes sense. You have 15 tiles, need to discard one to have 14 tiles (7 hand + 7 melded). Then you will be in a 13-tile hand needing one more to win. So you are not yet ready; you are choosing a discard to move toward readiness.  \n\nSo evaluate which discard leaves the best potential for high-value hand:  \n\nYour melds are both Dots, so aiming for Half Flush (honors + Dots only) is good. That means you should keep Dots and honors, discard suits W and T. You have no T, so discard W tiles.  \n\nAmong W tiles: W2, W3, W7. W2 and W3 are connected, good for chow potential. W7 is isolated. Discarding W7 keeps the W2-W3 sequence, which is good for flexibility.  \n\nAlso consider honors: F3 (West Wind) is both prevalent and seat wind, so keeping it could yield 4 points if you get a pung. J1 is Red Dragon, potential for Dragon Pung (2 points). So keep both honors.  \n\nDots: You have B1, B7, B8. B1 is terminal, already used in a Chow meld, but having an extra B1 could be useful for a pair or pung? But B1 is terminal, good for Pung of Terminals or Honors. B7 and B8 are connected, good for chow.  \n\nGiven Half Flush aim, you want to keep Dots and honors. So discard a W tile. Among W2, W3, W7, W7 is least useful because it's not connected and far from your Dots focus.  \n\nAlso check tile counts: W7 has 4 left, so not rare.  \n\nDiscarding W7 seems reasonable.  \n\nOther discards:  \n- Discard B1: loses a terminal, but you already have B1 in meld, so extra B1 not needed. However, B1 is Dots, so keeping it helps Half Flush. Better to keep.  \n- Discard B7 or B8: breaks the sequence, reduces Dots count.  \n- Discard F3 or J1: loses honor potential.  \n\nSo best discard is W7.  \n\nBut also consider defense: looking at history, opponents have discarded some honors and terminals. Your hand is not close to high-value patterns like All Honors etc. So offensive play is fine.  \n\nThus, discard W7.  \n\nReason: To aim for Half Flush (6 points) by keeping Dots and honors, discarding isolated Wan tile (W7) maintains the W2-W3 sequence for possible chow and keeps all Dots and honors for higher point potential.  \n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
